 "anatomy-steps": {
  "card/card-1": {
   "steps": [
    {
     "kind": "answer",
     "scope": "Elevation",
     "question": "What rises above the page?",
     "answer": "Cards too",
     "why": "Cards lift to feel like objects."
    },
    {
     "kind": "answer",
     "scope": "Elevation",
     "question": "What separates groups on the page?",
     "answer": "Cards",
     "why": "Cards hold each offer."
    },
    {
     "kind": "answer",
     "scope": "Shape",
     "question": "What should control corners say?",
     "answer": "Pill",
     "why": "Pill controls feel playful."
    },
    {
     "kind": "rule",
     "situation": "a card on the page",
     "use": "elevation.card (pronounced)",
     "not": "a flat card",
     "why": "Cards float to feel tactile."
    },
    {
     "kind": "token",
     "semantic": "color.surface.default",
     "key": "background",
     "value": "#FFFFFF",
     "darkValue": "#1F1411",
     "byScheme": true,
     "rule": "The base fill for content.",
     "pick": [
      "neutral.0: the base for content; body text reads ",
      {
       "ratio": 18
      },
      " on it, above the ",
      {
       "ratio": 4.5
      },
      " floor."
     ],
     "darkPick": [
      "neutral.50: the base for content; body text reads ",
      {
       "ratio": 17.12
      },
      " on it, above the ",
      {
       "ratio": 4.5
      },
      " floor."
     ],
     "shares": [
      "surface.raised",
      "action.secondary"
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "token",
     "semantic": "radius.surface",
     "key": "radius",
     "value": "1.5rem",
     "byScheme": true,
     "pick": [
      "radius.24: We use a 24px corner for cards, menus and dialogs in order to make each offer read as a separate object, in a bold tone by brand convention. A large radius does this by breaking the outline with a wide curve while the sides stay straight. So each offer reads as one friendly object, because containers get their own corner (shape.count) and the brand is bold (brand.q1)."
     ],
     "shares": []
    },
    {
     "kind": "token",
     "semantic": "space.inset.container",
     "key": "padding",
     "value": "2rem",
     "byScheme": true,
     "pick": [
      "space.32"
     ],
     "shares": []
    },
    {
     "kind": "token",
     "semantic": "color.border.default",
     "key": "border",
     "value": "#F2DCCF",
     "darkValue": "#4D3A33",
     "byScheme": true,
     "rule": "Quiet edges and dividers. Decorative, so it has no contrast minimum.",
     "pick": [
      "neutral.200: a quiet line that stays visible on every surface it meets (lowest ",
      {
       "ratio": 1.26
      },
      ", on surface.canvas). It decorates, so it holds no contrast minimum."
     ],
     "darkPick": [
      "neutral.200: a quiet line that stays visible on every surface it meets (lowest ",
      {
       "ratio": 1.69
      },
      ", on surface.default). It decorates, so it holds no contrast minimum."
     ],
     "shares": [
      "surface.pressed",
      "disabled.border"
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "token",
     "semantic": "border-width.default",
     "key": "border-width",
     "value": "2px",
     "byScheme": true,
     "pick": [
      "border-width.2"
     ],
     "shares": []
    },
    {
     "kind": "token",
     "semantic": "elevation.card",
     "key": "shadow",
     "value": "0px 12px 32px, #1F141124",
     "darkValue": "0px 12px 32px, #00000080",
     "byScheme": true,
     "pick": [
      "shadow.card: We use step 1 of the shadow scale for cards in order to show their height as objects above the page. The cast shade does this by a 12px offset and a 32px blur at 14%. So each offer feels like an object you can pick up, because cards rise too (elevation.roles) and shadows are pronounced (elevation.values)."
     ],
     "darkPick": [
      "shadow.card-dark: We use the light card shadow's shape in dark in order to show the same card height by the dark-shade relation. The cast shade does this by the same 12px offset and 32px blur, at 50% black instead of 14%. So each offer still reads as an object, though its shade reads weaker on dark than in light, because cards rise too (elevation.roles) and dark ships as standard (theming.values)."
     ],
     "shares": []
    }
   ]
  },
